## Schedule ordered machine operations

Exercise files: [flowshop.lp](../assets/scheduling/flowshop.lp) · [ins-01-3-2.lp](../assets/scheduling/instance.lp) · [jobshop.lp](../assets/scheduling/jobshop.lp).

```elixir
workdir = Course.workspace(Path.expand("../assets/scheduling", __DIR__))
```

Two related scheduling models share jobs, machines and operation durations. In permutation flow shop, every job visits machines in the same fixed order and every machine uses the same job permutation. In job shop, each job has its own input operation order and each machine may use a different job permutation.

For both variants, operations of one job obey their sequence and a machine processes at most one operation at a time. Minimize the total completion time. Keep the two formulations distinct when testing the provided instances.

### Read durations and operation order

`job/1`, `machine/1` and `duration/3` describe the shared input. Job shop additionally uses `sequence/3`. Preserve the templates’ `perm` output signatures when representing the selected processing order.

### Complete both encodings

Write the flow-shop and job-shop formulations separately. Start by validating feasible orders, then derive timing and minimize makespan. Compare proven optimal projected models with the corresponding reference solutions.

```elixir
Course.archive(workdir, "scheduling.zip")
```

```elixir
~S"""
#show perm/2.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "instance.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

```elixir
~S"""
#show perm/3.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "instance.lp")], models: :all)
|> Course.display()
```

