## Schedule tasks and workers

Exercise files: [instance-10.lp](../assets/task-planning/instances/instance-10.lp) · [instance-10.json](../assets/task-planning/solutions/instance-10.json) · [task-planning.lp](../assets/task-planning/task-planning.lp).

```elixir
workdir = Course.workspace(Path.expand("../assets/task-planning", __DIR__))
```

Choose start days and worker assignments within the planning horizon. A length-L task starting on day D finishes on D+L-1. Respect deadlines, strict precedence, resource capacities and holidays. Workers cannot serve overlapping tasks. A worker counts toward every category they possess.

Complete the starter encoding before comparing its projected models with the supplied solution data.

### Keep the data contract

Inputs use `final/1`, `task/3`, `before/2`, `uses/2`, `at_most/2`, `worker/2`, `requires/2`, `requires/3` and `holiday/2`. Show `starts/2` and `assign/2`. `requires/2` fixes the total worker count; `requires/3` sets a minimum for a category.

### Develop and check the encoding

Complete the editable encoding below. Compare its shown atom sets with the JSON answers in `solutions`; `optimal_cost` records a proven optimum when present. Download your edits before cleanup.

```elixir
~S"""
#show starts/2.
#show assign/2.
"""
|> Clingo.solve!(files: [Path.join(workdir, "instances/instance-10.lp")])
|> Course.display()
```

### Check boundary cases

Test the smallest instance first, then check each derived relation. Compare projected model sets and, when optimizing, proven costs.

